points.explanation:
In conversation the long 'must' forms are almost always shortened:

- {〜なければ} → {〜なきゃ}: {行[い]かなきゃ}
- {〜なくては} → {〜なくちゃ}: {行[い]かなくちゃ}
- {〜ないと(いけない)}: {行[い]かないと}

Very often the ending ({いけない} / {ならない}) is dropped completely, leaving just {もう行[い]かなきゃ} 'I've gotta go.' These dropped versions are casual.

{〜ないといけません} is the one you can use in polite conversation too; it's softer than {〜なければなりません} and very common at work: {確認[かくにん]しないといけませんね} 'We'll need to check that.'

{ても} means 'even if' or 'even though': the result in the main clause doesn't change, despite what you'd expect. {雨[あめ]が降[ふ]っても、行[い]きます} 'Even if it rains, I'll go.'

Forms:

- Verbs: {て}-form + {も}: {降[ふ]っても}, {食[た]べても}, {飲[の]んでも}
- {い}-adjectives: {〜くても}: {高[たか]くても}
- {な}-adjectives and nouns: {〜でも}: {静[しず]かでも}, {子[こ]どもでも}
- Negative: {〜なくても}

With question words it means 'no matter how / what / how many times': {いくら食[た]べても} 'no matter how much I eat', {何回[なんかい]聞[き]いても} 'however many times I listen', {どんなに忙[いそが]しくても} 'however busy'.

It works for real past events too: {薬[くすり]を飲[の]んでも、治[なお]らなかった} 'Even though I took medicine, it didn't get better.'
